include "../../linux.mereo"
include "../../core.mereo"

-- REJECTED on purpose: a span that claims more bytes than its backing has.
--
-- `line` is five bytes and the span says seventeen. Nothing about the span is
-- wrong at the moment it is made -- it is two numbers in a struct -- but every
-- access through it afterwards is bounded by a length that was never true, and
-- `find`, `last` and `search` all walk to `length` by definition. One lie at
-- adoption, inherited by every read that follows.
--
-- The requirement is declared on the resource itself, over its own fields:
--
--     span is
--       data is 8 bytes
--       length is 8 bytes
--       ensure length <= data.size
--
-- which is the same keyword and grammar a primitive uses for its contract, one
-- level out: there a clause on an in port constrains the CALL, here it
-- constrains the ADOPTION. Checked where the instance is made, because that is
-- where both numbers exist. Free -- the corpus is byte-identical with the
-- invariants declared.
--
-- It is also what the access analysis needs. It cannot assume
-- `length <= data.size` while nothing enforces it, and without that assumption
-- every `[v.data + i]` in a loop bounded by `v.length` stays unproved.

program goes
  line is "abcde"
  v is already span (data is line, length is 17)
  n is 0
  v.find (byte is 32, offset is n)
end
